  <!-- 03 — RESEARCH -->
  <div class="case-shell" id="research">
    <section class="case-section">
      <p class="sec-label">3. Research</p>
      <h2 class="case-heading">One problem, three types of user.</h2>
      <p class="case-body-text" style="margin-top:1.5rem;max-width:600px;">
        Most people rely on memory, bank statement scrolling, or informal notes to
        track subscriptions. The problem isn't awareness that subscriptions exist —
        it's the absence of a single source of truth.
      </p>

      <div class="challenge-pair" style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
        <div class="challenge-card challenge-card--neutral">
          <p class="challenge-card-label">User archetypes</p>
          <h3 class="challenge-card-title">Who uses SUBScout</h3>
          <ul class="challenge-list">
            <li><strong>The forgetter</strong> — pays for services they no longer use, discovers them only on bank statements.</li>
            <li><strong>The optimiser</strong> — actively wants to reduce spend but lacks a clear breakdown.</li>
            <li><strong>The sharer</strong> — splits subscriptions with family and needs to track shared costs.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="challenge-card challenge-card--violet">
          <p class="challenge-card-label">Key pain points</p>
          <h3 class="challenge-card-title">What needed to change</h3>
          <ul class="challenge-list" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.88);">
            <li>No consolidated view across apps, stores, and cards.</li>
            <li>Renewal surprises — charges hit before users notice.</li>
            <li>Cancellation friction — users forget where to cancel.</li>
            <li>Monthly total unknown until calculated manually.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--stroke-lavender);padding:1rem 1.5rem;margin-top:2.5rem;font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--muted);font-style:italic;max-width:640px;">
        "I don't even know what I'm paying for until I check my bank app at the end of the month and see charges I didn't expect."
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

